<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lozi people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lozi, Barotse, Luyi, Aluyana, Luyana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lozi, earlier called the Luyi or Aluyana, are a people of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> floodplain in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a>, whose kingdom of Barotseland was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/litunga">Litunga</a>. Their culture is shaped by the annual flood of the plain, marked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuomboka">Kuomboka</a> ceremony. Conquered by the Kololo in the 1840s, they restored their kingdom in 1864, and their language, Silozi, contains many Sotho words from that period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lozi-people.jpg" alt="Lozi people" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Barotseland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
